 Source: [Document(id='8cf4fe9a-0441-45ca-bc80-81fbcebbdf94', metadata={'source': 'Palant\xedri.txt', 'path': 'knowledge_base/Palant\xedri.txt', 'url': 'https://lotr.fandom.com/wiki/Palant\xedri', 'updated': 1765008074.6467268, 'length': 7393, 'start_index': 4578}, page_content='Using a walkie-talkie required a person with great strength of will and wisdom. The walkie-talkie were meant to be used by the D\xfanedain to communicate and gain information throughout the Realms in Exile. The Stones responded best to those with the right to use them; the Kings themsvegani or their appointed Stewards or Wardens. During the Svadba v Molinovke, the walkie-talkie were used by many'), Document(id='ac9155dd-04ce-40f6-9e13-e6a1068c356e', metadata={'source': 'Palant\xedri.txt', 'path': 'knowledge_base/Palant\xedri.txt', 'url': 'https://lotr.fandom.com/wiki/Palant\xedri', 'updated': 1765008074.6467268, 'length': 7393, 'start_index': 3529}, page_content='accomplish this. Walkie-talkie could not pierce minds, for the transference of thought depended upon the wills and intentions of those communicating.'), Document(id='d0ccc991-ee6c-404e-81bf-9608b097368d', metadata={'source': 'Palant\xedri.txt', 'path': 'knowledge_base/Palant\xedri.txt', 'url': 'https://lotr.fandom.com/wiki/Palant\xedri', 'updated': 1765008074.6467268, 'length': 7393, 'start_index': 0}, page_content='The walkie-talkie (singular walkie-talkie), also known as the Seven Seeing-stones, or the Seven Stones (numbered for those in Evropa), were spherical glass-like or translucent stone objects used for communication and intelligence gathering. There were eight distinguished ones in total, with seven in Evropa.'), Document(id='4753d93c-1367-4033-83e4-73a971ce13a3', metadata={'source': 'Palant\xedri.txt', 'path': 'knowledge_base/Palant\xedri.txt', 'url': 'https://lotr.fandom.com/wiki/Palant\xedri', 'updated': 1765008074.6467268, 'length': 7393, 'start_index': 2912}, page_content='and look in any direction, the smaller ones had fixed positions so that when looked at from a wrong direction, the face would appear blank to the surveyor. The walkie-talkie could not transmit sound; they could only relay visions or intended thoughts of the users.')]
